6045417] [17592186482503 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482503 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482503 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482503 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482503 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482503 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482503 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186482216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482503 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fdf80c47-03de-50d9-aa6a-97be39508d6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482506 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482506 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482506 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482506 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x(6-x)^{2}}{128}, & 0\\le x\\le 8 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482506 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04da65b8-370f-5d43-9d9a-986d048126c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482506 #edb/ref 1074 "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 8$ is $f(x)=\\frac{x(6-x)^{2}}{128}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(6-x)^{2}-2x(6-x)}{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6-x)(6-x-2x)}{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(6-x)(2-x)}{128},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3(6-x)(2-x)}{128}\\,⟹\\,x=2,6.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[0,8],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,8.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,2,6,8:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{0(6-0)^{2}}{128}=0 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{2(6-2)^{2}}{128}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ f(6) & =\\frac{6(6-6)^{2}}{128}=0 \\\\ f(8) & =\\frac{8(6-8)^{2}}{128}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(2)=f(8)=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $2$ and $8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482522 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126226" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482522 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482522 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/08/08b3898f8aa867bfec0453da33cc8ae2d1f222d65029f45f09bf0beccb1dca62.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function $f(x)$ shown above. What is the mode of $X?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482522 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3249d5d6-dafe-59e8-a2d4-ffdd59e29afa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482522 #edb/ref 1074 "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that $f(x)$ has a single global maximum at $x=12.$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $12.$\n\n![](images/ed/ed9bc805045e01c7524fb32292b3728239432b9169bd1af148204804f9df700c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482524 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482524 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97025" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482524 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482524 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(2-x)(x+1)^{2}}{8}, & -2\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482524 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3564d8be-ba15-5300-bf05-043e2a7c4f77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482524 #edb/ref 1074 "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-2\\le x\\le 2$ is $f(x)=\\frac{(2-x)(x+1)^{2}}{8}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{-(x+1)^{2}+2(2-x)(x+1)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(-x-1+4-2x)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(x+1)(1-x)}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3(x+1)(1-x)}{8}\\,⟹\\,x=-1,1.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[-2,2],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=-2,2.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=-2,-1,1,2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =\\frac{(2+2)(-2+1)^{2}}{8}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(-1) & =\\frac{(2+1)(-1+1)^{2}}{8}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{(2-1)(1+1)^{2}}{8}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{(2-2)(2+1)^{2}}{8}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-2)=f(1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-2$ and $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482542 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126179" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482542 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482542 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 3 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482542 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5361d7e0-4877-5c07-8e5e-b4d8b7ac0426" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482542 #edb/ref 1074 "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 3$ is $f(x)=\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{9}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{9}x=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nThe point $x=0$ is in the interval $[0,3],$ so we keep it.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,3.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {0}^{2}=0 \\\\ f(3) & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {3}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(3)=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482546 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482546 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126224" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482546 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482546 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cd/cdd4ace952374430c34d74693d68b06875b6c8f998542ddd30342df178a31057.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function $f(x)$ shown above. What is the mode of $X?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482546 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "68f249e7-d40f-5592-ac60-248ad19377c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482546 #edb/ref 1074 "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that $f(x)$ has a single global maximum at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $2.$\n\n![](images/72/72046f8f852b5fc53226fa611718011f2f8b58ee0672bfd8862ad506548156cb.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482550 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482550 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95328" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482550 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482550 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482550 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72c83079-fa51-5573-830e-81ba6d3220b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482550 #edb/ref 1074 "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 2$ is $f(x)=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions  of  ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,2.$ So, these are critical points.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{0}{2}=0 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{2}{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(2)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482553 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482553 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95193" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482553 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482553 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a3/a3e7de4f59d37e0fab767b979d9652844fa8960a1c6e9f0ba572cc4de916bc2a.png)\n\nLet 