2186045417] [17592186482573 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482573 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/aa/aa2638a96ae91d0b2a8a0d1216e8256c7823c231c5ceecd9a4b3fd14c239ed53.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function $f(x)$ shown above. What is the mode of $X?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482573 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a5a77e1a-841f-57c5-8990-3f339cc0802e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482573 #edb/ref 1074 "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that $f(x)$ has a single global maximum at $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $4.$\n\n![](images/52/52c2514b1970553a6b4f80138e4baa6b8384d482caa447b95bd07dde5c252d11.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482588 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482588 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126721" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482588 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482588 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482588 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c069136c-9e24-5901-bcc3-c8fd76fb24b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482588 #edb/ref 1074 "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $x\\ge 0$ is $f(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{(1+x)^{3}}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions of  ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoint $x=0.$ So, this is the only critical point.\n\nSince ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for $x>0,$ then $f(0)$ is the global maximum of $f(x).$\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482592 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482592 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95380" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482592 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482592 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{2}, & -1\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482592 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc6f0c6d-fbfc-515f-9faf-cdb2e36bd29a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482592 #edb/ref 1074 "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-1\\le x\\le 1$ is $f(x)=\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =10{x}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n10{x}^{3}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nThis critical point is within the interval $[-1,1],$ so we keep it.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=-1,1.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=-1,0,1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =\\frac{5\\cdot (-1)^{4}}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ f(0) & =\\frac{5\\cdot {0}^{4}}{2}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{5\\cdot {1}^{4}}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-1)=f(1)=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-1$ and $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482595 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482595 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95191" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482595 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482595 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7c/7c41fd3e5730b563acdd30f13853b1dfa5111cb16fb8b7f32fa746f58b515538.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown above. What are the modes of $X?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482595 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d092f972-98cf-5c56-8546-b76547cbb36a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482595 #edb/ref 1074 "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that the global maxima of $f(x)$ are at $x=1,5,10.$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $1,5$ and $10.$\n\n![](images/d8/d82edc89a519c6286f665c217871965b889697e87c3a4d6176f2e29e8b7596ac.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482596 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482596 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482596 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482596 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{100}(5+4x-{x}^{2}), & 0\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482596 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d0fd2e28-4af0-53c5-9c68-348af97ab34c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482596 #edb/ref 1074 "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 5$ is $f(x)=\\frac{3}{100}(5+4x-{x}^{2}).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3}{100}(4-2x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{100}(4-2x)=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nThe point $x=2$ is in the interval $[0,5],$ so we keep it.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,5.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,2,5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{3}{100}(5+4\\cdot 0-{0}^{2})=\\frac{15}{100}=\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{3}{100}(5+4\\cdot 2-{2}^{2})=\\frac{27}{100} \\\\ f(5) & =\\frac{3}{100}(5+4\\cdot 5-{5}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(2)=\\frac{27}{100}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482610 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482610 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126294" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482610 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482610 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f6/f6f2ce8adb8c1b35c2fd4928805f486920228b913c3a7fc18ddc60b26a8e8fcd.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown above. What are the modes of $X?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482610 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed1f11b0-312f-5ca9-81fd-de20b37867fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482610 #edb/ref 1074 "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that the global maxima of $f(x)$ are at $x=5,7.$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $5$ and $7.$\n\n![](images/59/59b72efa72dd48bc2fb74fed5f795341f59af377af0756965abab9d23390d468.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482617 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482617 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127090" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482617 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482617 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{96}{595}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-2), & -1\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482617 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fed1e82d-9029-565f-bbd7-a48e9974d84e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482617 #edb/ref 1074 "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-1\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2}$ is $f(x)=-\\frac{96}{595}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-2).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{96}{595}(4{x}^{3}-4x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{384}{595}x({x}^{2}-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{384}{595}x({x}^{2}-1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[-1,\\frac{3}{2}],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=\\pm 1.$ So, these are critical points as well, but $-1$ is included above.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,\\pm 1,\\frac{3}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((-1)^{4}-2(-1)^{2}-2)=\\frac{288}{595} \\\\ f(0) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((0)^{4}-2(0)^{2}-2)=\\frac{192}{595} \\\\ f(1) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}-2)=\\frac{288}{595} \\\\ f(\\frac{3}{2}) & =-\\frac{96}{595}({(\\frac{3}{2})}^{4}-2{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2)=\\frac{138}{595}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-1)=f(1)=\\frac{288}{595}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-1$ and $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
